 r"I am writing to apply for the AI Analyst position with BlueBay Asset Management at RBC in London. I recently completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have worked on building AI workflows for the people who use them, testing whether their output can be trusted, and explaining the result to people who are not technical.",

 r"What attracts me most to this role is that the work has a visible end point: a portfolio manager or trader making a better decision because a tool built with them actually fits their process. The posting asks for someone who knows how to evaluate whether AI output is reliable enough to act on, and that question has been at the centre of my recent work. I enjoy taking an unclear workflow, understanding what the user really needs, and turning it into something that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern on a medical AI product for a pharmaceutical client. I designed a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension rubric with red-line rules, which the client adopted as their acceptance standard, then built a Python pipeline calling six LLM APIs with retrieval and search augmentation and scored more than 2,400 responses with an LLM-as-judge framework. I also ran blinded A/B tests of a medically reviewed knowledge graph. The lesson was that a model which sounds confident is not the same as a model which is right, and that the evaluation has to be designed before anyone looks at the answers.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I analyse merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python and build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations and commercial teams use. When my supervisor asked me to configure a merchant campaign by hand, I worked through a few examples, saw the underlying logic, and used Claude Code to build a multi-step agent with a validation step to check its own output. It cut the time the task took by around 40 to 50 percent. The difficult part was not the code but deciding which steps the agent should own and how to prove to a sceptical colleague that it was accurate, which is close to the embedded, desk-by-desk work this role describes.",

 r"My MSc dissertation applied predictive and causal machine learning to 458,840 UK Biobank participants, with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding, and was delivered as an interactive Streamlit application. It gave me the statistical foundation to judge when a result is strong enough to act on, and taught me to report a weak or null finding honestly rather than dress it up.",

 r"I should be clear about one gap. I have not yet worked in financial markets or with investment data, so I would be learning how portfolio managers and traders work in my first months rather than arriving with that knowledge. I learn new domains quickly, as the move from biology to consumer technology to medical AI shows, and the evaluation and workflow-building skills transfer directly.",

 r"I would bring RBC strong Python and experience building against LLM APIs, hands-on RAG and agentic workflow experience, regular hands-on use of Claude Code, and the habit of measuring whether an AI tool actually works before recommending it. I have the right to work in the UK until January 2029 without sponsorship. I am based in London and am happy to work four days a week from the Bishopsgate office.",

 r"Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience in AI evaluation and workflow building could help BlueBay's investment teams.",
